# Message Queues vs. Publish-Subscribe

> **TL;DR:** A **queue** gives each message to **one** receiver. **Pub-Sub** gives each message to **every** subscriber.

---

## The Simple Idea

Both are ways for parts of a system to send messages to each other without calling each other directly. The difference is **who receives each message**.

| | Message Queue | Pub-Sub |
|---|---|---|
| **Who gets the message** | One receiver | All subscribers |
| **Real-life analogy** | Bank line with several tellers. Each customer is served by one teller. | Radio station. It broadcasts once and everyone tuned in hears it. |

---

## Message Queue

Producers put messages in a queue. Consumers take them out in order (first in, first out).

```
Producer  →  [ M3 | M2 | M1 ]  →  Consumer A  (gets M1)
                               →  Consumer B  (gets M2)
```

**How it works**
- Each message is processed by only one consumer.
- If a consumer is offline, the message waits in the queue until someone is ready.

**Good**
- Reliable delivery
- Keeps order
- Spreads work across many workers

**Bad**
- Not made for sending the same message to many receivers

**Used for:** task distribution, background jobs, load balancing

**Example:** 1,000 images need resizing. Put them in a queue and 5 workers share the work, with each image resized once.

---

## Publish-Subscribe (Pub-Sub)

Publishers send messages to a **topic** (a channel). Subscribers listen to the topics they care about.

```
                       →  Subscriber A  (gets a copy)
Publisher  →  [Topic]  →  Subscriber B  (gets a copy)
                       →  Subscriber C  (gets a copy)
```

**How it works**
- Every subscriber to that topic gets a copy.
- Publishers and subscribers don't know about each other.

**Good**
- One message reaches many receivers
- Subscribers can be added or removed easily

**Bad**
- An offline subscriber may miss messages
- Messages can be duplicated
- Messages may arrive out of order

**Used for:** real-time notifications, broadcasting, event-driven systems

**Example:** A user places an order. The email service, inventory service, and analytics service all receive the same "order placed" message.

---

## Main Differences

| Aspect | Message Queue | Pub-Sub |
|---|---|---|
| **Communication** | Point-to-point | One-to-many or many-to-many |
| **Delivery** | One consumer per message | Every subscriber gets a copy |
| **Order** | First in, first out | Not always guaranteed |
| **Offline receiver** | Message waits | May miss the message (depends on tool) |
| **Best for** | Sharing work | Telling many parts what happened |

---

## Extra Points to Know

- **They can be combined.** A common setup is Pub-Sub to broadcast an event, with each subscriber having its own queue. Each one gets a copy and processes it at its own speed.
- **"May miss messages" depends on the tool.** Kafka stores messages so late subscribers can catch up. Simple Pub-Sub tools don't.
- **Queues can lose order** when several consumers work in parallel, even though they are first in, first out.
- **Dead Letter Queue (DLQ):** a place for messages that keep failing, so you can inspect them later.
- **Idempotency:** duplicates can happen in both, so your code should handle the same message twice without harm.

---

## Common Tools

| Type | Tools |
|---|---|
| **Queues** | RabbitMQ, AWS SQS |
| **Pub-Sub** | Google Pub/Sub, AWS SNS |
| **Both** | Kafka |

---

## When to Use Which

| Situation | Choose |
|---|---|
| One job should be done by exactly one worker | **Queue** |
| Many services need to react to the same event | **Pub-Sub** |
| Both needs together | **Pub-Sub feeding a queue for each subscriber** |
